   "subject": "Same population, but with a few distinct items",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "Hi,<div><br></div><div>Suppose we have the data from two exams applied to the same population:</div><div><b>Exam A:</b> Has items 1 to 5 and 11 to 40.</div><div><b>Exam B:</b> Has items 6 to 10 and 11 to 40.</div><div><br></div><div>Each individual answered exactly one of the exams (ie. individual 1 = answered exam A, individual 2 = answered exam B, individual 3 = answered exam B...)</div><div><br></div><div>How would I go about estimating the 3PL IRT parameters for these items using MIRT?</div><div><br></div><div>Best,</div><div>Pedro</div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "ys081mAyAwAJ",
   "from": "Hugo Harada",
   "author": "73fb61795976b321",
   "date": "2021-02-24T13:35:39Z",
   "subject": "Re: Same population, but with a few distinct items",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\">Pedro,</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\">Yes, use the multigroup function and organize your data in 50 columns. Group A, who answered exam A, should be have only NA in colums 6-10, Group B, should have NAs in columns 1-5. You will also need a column to identify to which group each individual belongs to.</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\">Good luck,</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\">Hugo</div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div>"
    ],
